-- title:
--   Isomorphic regluings have cohomologous tangent cocycles
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a commutative local artinian ring $B$ whose residue field $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B$ is algebraically closed, and a commutative $B$-algebra $B_1$.
--
--   *The small extension.* The hypotheses on $B \to B_1$ are: `hπ`, that `algebraMap B B₁` is surjective; `hker`, that its kernel $I =$ `RingHom.ker (algebraMap B B₁)` is a nilpotent ideal; `hsmall`, that $I \cdot \mathfrak m_B = \bot$; `hI`, that $I \le \mathfrak m_B$; and `hq`, that the image of $q$ in $B$ is nilpotent.
--
--   *The object over $B_1$.* A scheme $A_1$ with a morphism $f_1 : A_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} B_1$, a relative group law $L_1$ on $f_1$ (functorial multiplication, unit, inverse on points of $f_1$ over arbitrary bases, with the group axioms and naturality), together with `hc₁`, that $L_1$ is commutative, and `h₁`, the bundle `AbelianSchemePropertyBundle B₁ f₁` ($f_1$ smooth and proper, with connected fibres and a relative group law).
--
--   *Formal-group data.* A commutative two‑dimensional formal group law $F$ over $B$; a system $\theta_1$ of formal coordinates for $f_1$ in two variables, with `hθ₁` asserting that $\theta_1$ presents the infinitesimal points of $L_1$ by the base change $F \otimes_B B_1$ (compatibility with $B_1$-algebra maps applied to nilpotent coordinate tuples, and, for every ideal $J$ with $J^{n+1} = \bot$, that $\theta_1$ is a bijection from $J$-valued coordinate tuples onto the $J$-infinitesimal points, carrying the truncated formal addition $F$ to the group law); `hinf₁`, that for every $B_1$-algebra $C$, every $m$ and every point $P$ of $f_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C$ killed by $q^m$ there is a nilpotent ideal $J \subseteq C$ with $P$ being $J$-infinitesimal; and `hheight`, that for every field $k$ and every surjective ring homomorphism $\rho : B \to k$ the quotient of $k[[X_0,X_1]]$ by the ideal generated by the components of the $q$-th iterate series `(F.map ρ).nthSeries q` has $k$-dimension $q^4$.
--
--   *The tangent module.* A module $V$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B$, finite over it, also a $B$-module compatibly (with the opposite action central), and a $B$-linear map $\iota : V \to B$ which is injective (`hι`) and whose range is exactly $I$ viewed as a $B$-submodule of $B$ (`hιI`).
--
--   *The base deformation and its charts.* A bare deformation $D_0$ of $(f_1, L_1)$ over $B$ — that is, a scheme $D_0.A$ with a morphism $D_0.f$ to $\operatorname{Spec} B$, a commutative relative group law $D_0.L$, the abelian-scheme property bundle, and a morphism $D_0.g : A_1 \to D_0.A$ making $A_1$ the base change of $D_0.A$ along $\operatorname{Spec} B_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ and carrying $L_1$ to $D_0.L$ — with $D_0.f$ separated. Further: an ordered affine cover $\mathcal U$ of $D_0.A$ (a finite linearly ordered family of affine opens covering $D_0.A$), a distinguished index $i_0$, a morphism $e_0 : \operatorname{Spec} B \to \mathcal U.U\,i_0$ whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of $D_0.L$ (`he₀`), and likewise $e_1 : \operatorname{Spec} \mathrm{ResidueField}\,B \to (\mathcal U \text{ base-changed}).U\,i_0$ whose composite with the open immersion is the unit section of the base-changed group law `RelativeGroupLaw.baseChange (specMap B (ResidueField B)) D₀.L` (`he₁`). For each index $s$ of a strictly increasing pair, a ring isomorphism $\sigma_s : \mathrm{ResidueField}\,B \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s) \cong \Gamma$ of the corresponding overlap of the base-changed cover, subject to `hσ₁` (on elements $1 \otimes x$, $\sigma_s$ is the pullback map along `pullback.fst` followed by restriction) and `hσ₂` (on elements $a \otimes 1$, $\sigma_s$ is the structure algebra map of the overlap).
--
--   *The two cocycles.* Two elements $c$ and $c'$ of [`Algebra.PointDerivations`](def/Algebra_PointDerivations.html#L9) over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B$ of the ring $\Gamma$ of the base-changed chart $(\mathcal U \text{ base-changed}).U\,i_0$, at the evaluation homomorphism determined by $e_1$, with values in $\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B}(V^\vee, C^1)$, where $C^1$ is the degree‑$1$ Čech cochain group of the $\mathcal O$-module presheaf `OModulePresheaf.unit (pullback.snd D₀.f (specMap B (ResidueField B)))` on the base-changed cover; thus each is a residue-field-linear map $D$ with $D(ab) = \mathrm{ev}(a)\, D(b) + \mathrm{ev}(b)\, D(a)$. The hypotheses `hc` and `hc'` assert that for all $a$ and all $\xi \in V^\vee$ the cochains $c\,a\,\xi$ and $c'\,a\,\xi$ lie in the kernel of the Čech differential in degree $1$, i.e. are $1$-cocycles.
--
--   *The two regluings.* Two families $\tau, \tau'$ of self-isomorphisms of the overlaps $\mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s$, and two bare deformations $D, D'$ with `hD : D₀.IsRegluingBy 𝒰 τ D` and `hD' : D₀.IsRegluingBy 𝒰 τ' D'`; each such predicate says that the automorphisms are compatible with the morphism to $\operatorname{Spec} B$ and fix the restrictions of $D_0.g$, and that there are open immersions of the charts $\mathcal U.U\,i$ into the new total space, over $D_0.f$, jointly surjective, compatible with $D_0.g$, and matching on each overlap after insertion of the corresponding automorphism.
--
--   The hypotheses `hτ` and `hτ'` say that $c$, respectively $c'$, records the tangent coordinates of these automorphisms: for each $s$ there is a family $c_s$ of residue-field-linear maps from $V^\vee$ to $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B \otimes_B \Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s)$, indexed by the sections of the base-changed chart, satisfying [`AlgebraicGeometry.SmallExtension.IsTangentCoordsOfPairAt`](def/AlgebraicGeometry_TangentCoordsOfPairAt.html#L31) for the ideal $I$, the module $V$, the map $\iota$, the ring $\Gamma(D_0.A, \mathcal U.\mathrm{inter}\,s)$ and the pair of morphisms consisting of the canonical `fromSpec` of that affine overlap and of its twist by $(\tau_s)$, respectively $(\tau'_s)$, followed by the open immersion — that is, there exist a morphism $w_0$ from the spectrum of the thickening $(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B \otimes_B \Gamma) \otimes_{\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B} \mathrm{TrivSqZeroExt}(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B, V)$ to the special fibre, lying over the prescribed base morphism, and a lift $w_1$ of its translate by the group law into the chart $(\mathcal U \text{ base-changed}).U\,i_0$, such that $w_0$ composed with the map to the special fibre is a tangent of the given pair in the sense of `IsTangentOfPair` (it factors through a Schlessinger map out of the pair ring of $I$) and $c_s$ equals the tangent-coordinate function of the chart ring homomorphism attached to $w_1$ — and, in addition, $\sigma_s(c_s\,a\,\xi)$ equals the $s$-component of $c\,a\,\xi$, respectively of $c'\,a\,\xi$, for all $a$ and $\xi$.
--
--   *The isomorphism.* Finally `hiso : D.IsIso D'`, an isomorphism $e : D.A \cong D'.A$ with $e \circ D'.f = D.f$ and $D.g$ followed by $e$ equal to $D'.g$.
--
--   The conclusion is: for every section $a$ of the base-changed chart $(\mathcal U \text{ base-changed}).U\,i_0$ and every $\xi \in \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathrm{ResidueField}\,B}(V, \mathrm{ResidueField}\,B)$ there exists a Čech $0$-cochain $b$ of the presheaf `OModulePresheaf.unit (pullback.snd D₀.f (specMap B (ResidueField B)))` on the base-changed cover whose image under the degree-$0$ Čech differential equals $c\,a\,\xi - c'\,a\,\xi$. In other words, the two $1$-cocycles attached to $\tau$ and $\tau'$ differ by a coboundary, componentwise in $a$ and $\xi$.
--
--   This is the injectivity half of the Čech-cohomological classification of regluings of a smooth proper group scheme along a small extension of artinian local rings: isomorphic regluings produce cohomologous tangent cocycles, so that the class of the cocycle depends only on the isomorphism class of the deformation. It is obtained by combining the comparison of charts attached to an isomorphism of regluings (`exists_chartIso_comp_eq_of_isRegluingBy_of_isIso`) with the coboundary computation from such a chart comparison (`exists_d_eq_sub_of_chartIso_comp_eq_of_isTangentCoordsOfPairAt`), and feeds the construction of formal coordinates lifting those of the fibre over $B_1$.
